        <td class="NewsText" style="font-size: 15px">Red Hat, Inc. has announced the release of <a href="redhat">Red Hat</a> Enterprise Linux (RHEL) 10.3 and 9.9. Besides the usual round of software application updates, these two new version also introduce new tools to combat the increasingly ominous AI-driven security threats: "<span class="Quote">Today, we are proud to announce the availability of Red Hat Enterprise Linux (RHEL) 10.3 and RHEL 9.9. Today's launch is about reinforcing the platform's foundation to meet the evolving security, operational, and AI-driven demands of the modern enterprise. RHEL 10.3 and 9.9 deliver on this necessity by integrating zero trust architectures, and strengthening digital sovereignty, all while providing AI-augmented, production-ready diagnostic tools and upholding the uncompromising stability our customers expect. AI-driven threats move with unprecedented speed, which means traditional security is no longer enough. Red Hat has shifted toward a model of proactive resilience, where security is fundamentally integrated with intelligence. By using AI-augmented diagnostics to predict vulnerabilities and implement automated, intelligent remediation, we are defending infrastructure while actively hardening it against the next generation of sophisticated threats.</span>" See the <a href="https://www.redhat.com/en/blog/introducing-red-hat-enterprise-linux-103-and-99">release announcement</a> and the release notes (<a href="https://docs.redhat.com/en/documentation/red_hat_enterprise_linux/10/html/10.3_release_notes/index">10.3</a> and <a href="https://docs.redhat.com/en/documentation/red_hat_enterprise_linux/9/html/9.9_release_notes/index">9.9</a>) for more information. Both RHEL 10.3 (<a href="table.php?distribution=redhat&pkglist=true&version=rhel-10.3#pkglist">pkglist</a>) and RHEL 9.9 (<a href="table.php?distribution=redhat&pkglist=true&version=rhel-9.9#pkglist">pkglist</a>) are available for download from Red Hat's <a href="https://access.redhat.com/">Customer</a> and <a href="https://developers.redhat.com/">Developer</a> portals.</td>
